Hypothesis 1.) The frontend is structurally organized, but its event and state pipelines are not yet verified at each handoff.

A.) Add a dedicated pipeline behavior spec at tests/pipeline.spec.js instead of expanding structural audits. Verify HomeView open-student-menu -> App state -> StudentNavigation/CalendarView rendering, and close-student-menu -> home rendering.
B.) Verify CalendarView month navigation in both directions, including wraparound across the 16-month range and clearing an active day-cell animation when the month changes.
C.) Verify day-cell guards and timing as observable states: a valid September cell starts the explosion, the replacement appears after the transition, the animation finishes, and empty or non-September cells do nothing. Use state-based Playwright assertions with bounded timeouts instead of exact timer sleeps.
D.) Keep each pipeline test named after one handoff or state transition so a failure identifies the owning boundary immediately. Run the focused pipeline spec, then the existing structure suite and build.

Hypothesis 2.) CalendarView currently owns calendar data generation, visual-theme synchronization, month state, event translation, and animation cleanup; splitting it immediately would add wiring before a failing ownership boundary is demonstrated.
A.) Do not refactor CalendarView before the behavior tests establish which responsibility is difficult to diagnose or change.
B.) If the tests expose a real boundary, extract one responsibility at a time, starting with the pure calendar-month model, then theme synchronization, then day-cell animation lifecycle. Preserve the existing props and emitted events at each step.
C.) Re-run the focused pipeline behavior test after every extraction, followed by the relevant static audit and production build. Keep assets excluded from stale, duplicate, and consistency searches.

Recommendation.) The next implementation step should be the focused pipeline behavior spec. Refactoring CalendarView should follow only when that test evidence identifies a concrete ownership problem; the current structure and passing build do not justify a broad rewrite.
